  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Wednesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Upper level ridging to the east and \ntroughing to the west continue to funnel moisture into the Great \nBasin area. This will result in continued rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms, mainly in central and eastern Nevada today before \nexpanding to the entire forecast through Thursday. Thursday is \nalso expected to be the wettest day, with widespread accumulations\nof 0.25\u201d and a band of 0.5\u201d to 0.75\u201d shaping up just south of the\nUS-50 corridor. This has resulted in a Slight (15%) risk of \nexcessive rainfall for tomorrow and isolated flash flooding is \nexpected with the strongest cells. \n\nThe trough begins to lift to the north on Friday, weakening the \nmoisture flow for the weekend and mostly cutting off storms, \nleaving about a 10% to 20% chance through Sunday. Models remain in\ndisagreement after Sunday, with the biggest question being the \nfuture of the current monsoon event. The general indication is \nstill for the monsoon to continue in central Nevada, however the \nstrength and northern extent of the event still depend on how the \nupper ridge behaves over the next few days and into next week. The\ncurrent forecast is reflecting the more cut off scenario, with \ndry conditions Monday and Tuesday and some isolated showers for \nWednesday.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence in showers and\nthunderstorms through Friday, with moderate confidence in spatial\nextent of the heaviest accumulations. Moderate confidence in a\nmostly dry weekend. Low confidence in the forecast after Sunday as\nmodels continue to strongly disagree regarding the upper level\npattern. No changes to NBM forecast.",
